    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/atomic--test-atomic",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves that every atomic primitive (compare-and-set, arithmetic, load/store, bitwise, and single-bit operations on both scalar and pointer targets, plus the ATOMIC_DEFINE bit-array) performs the documented read-modify-write semantics: each call returns the prior value of the target and leaves the target updated to the expected result, including for negative operands and per-bit operations across the full atomic word width.\nTest steps:\n- Invoke each atomic API in turn against a known target value.\n- For arithmetic operations, exercise both positive and negative operands.\n- For bit operations, iterate over every bit position of the atomic word.\n- For ATOMIC_DEFINE, set and clear each bit of a multi-word bit array.\nExpected result:\n- Each call returns the target's previous value and the target holds the expected post-operation value.\nSee also: atomic_cas(), atomic_add(), atomic_sub(), atomic_inc(), atomic_dec(), atomic_get(), atomic_set(), atomic_clear(), atomic_or(), atomic_and(), atomic_xor(), atomic_nand(), atomic_test_bit(), atomic_test_and_clear_bit(), atomic_test_and_set_bit(), atomic_clear_bit(), atomic_set_bit(), ATOMIC_DEFINE",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "atomic__test_atomic"
        }
      ],
      "name": "atomic__test_atomic",
      "summary": "Verify each atomic operation API mutates and returns the expected value."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/atomic--test-atomic-overflow",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The C standard leaves signed overflow undefined, but Zephyr's atomic_t must behave deterministically. Passing proves that incrementing an atomic_t past its maximum value produces the same wrapped result as an ordinary increment of an equivalently-typed scalar, both at the signed maximum and at the all-ones (unsigned wrap to zero) boundary. This guarantees an atomic variable can be reasoned about with plain arithmetic where it is logically safe to do so.\nTest steps:\n- Seed an atomic_t and a matching scalar at the maximum signed value, increment both, and compare.\n- Seed both at the all-ones value (-1), increment both, and compare.\nExpected result:\n- After each increment the atomic value matches the plain-increment result and the expected wrapped boundary value.\nSee also: atomic_inc()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "atomic__test_atomic_overflow"
        }
      ],
      "name": "atomic__test_atomic_overflow",
      "summary": "Verify atomic increment overflow wraps identically to plain increment."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/atomic--test-threads-access-atomic",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves that atomic_inc() is safe under preemptive concurrency: when multiple equal-priority preemptible threads increment a shared atomic_t while being time-sliced in and out, no increment is lost to a torn read-modify-write, so the final value equals the exact total number of increments performed.\nTest steps:\n- Enable time slicing so equal-priority preemptible threads are scheduled fairly.\n- Start THREADS_NUM threads, each incrementing the shared atomic TEST_CYCLE times with a busy-wait between increments to force interleaving across slices.\n- Join all threads and disable time slicing.\nExpected result:\n- The shared atomic equals TEST_CYCLE * THREADS_NUM.\nSee also: atomic_inc()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "atomic__test_threads_access_atomic"
        }
      ],
      "name": "atomic__test_threads_access_atomic",
      "summary": "Verify atomic increments from concurrent threads are not lost."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/bitfield--test-bitfield",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Exercises the memory-mapped bit manipulation helpers across every bit of a word and of a large multi-byte bitfield. Passing proves each primitive sets, clears, tests and atomically test-and-set/clears only the addressed bit, returns the prior bit state, and leaves neighbouring bits untouched on both endiannesses.\nTest steps:\n- For each bit 0..31 of a 32-bit word: set, test, clear and re-test the bit, then exercise sys_test_and_set_bit() and sys_test_and_clear_bit() checking returned prior state and resulting value.\n- For each bit of a BITFIELD_SIZE-bit array: repeat the same sequence using the sys_bitfield_* helpers, validating the affected byte via BIT_INDEX/BIT_VAL.\nExpected result:\n- Every set/clear/test reflects the expected bit value and every test-and-modify call reports the correct previous state.\nSee also: sys_set_bit()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "bitfield__test_bitfield"
        }
      ],
      "name": "bitfield__test_bitfield",
      "summary": "Verify single-bit and bitfield set/clear/test primitives operate on the correct bit."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/clock--test-clock-cycle-32",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves that the architecture's 32-bit hardware cycle counter (k_cycle_get_32()) is a monotonically increasing time source whose rate is consistent with the tick-derived uptime clock (k_uptime_get_32()): the number of cycles elapsed over one observed millisecond of uptime corresponds to at least one millisecond worth of cycles and at least one millisecond when converted to nanoseconds, cross-validating the cycle counter against uptime.\nTest steps:\n- Align to a millisecond boundary and spin until the cycle counter advances, guarding against counter wrap-around.\n- Sample the cycle counter, align to a millisecond boundary, and spin until the 32-bit uptime advances by one millisecond.\n- Compute the cycle delta over that interval (skipping if the counter wrapped).\nExpected result:\n- The cycle delta exceeds one millisecond of cycles and, converted via k_cyc_to_ns_floor64(), exceeds one millisecond in nanoseconds.\nSee also: k_cycle_get_32(), k_uptime_get_32()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "clock__test_clock_cycle_32"
        }
      ],
      "name": "clock__test_clock_cycle_32",
      "summary": "Verify the 32-bit cycle counter advances consistently with uptime."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/clock--test-clock-cycle-64",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves that, on platforms providing a 64-bit cycle counter, the 64-bit and 32-bit cycle counters measure the same elapsed time over a short sleep: the 64-bit delta is at least as large as the 32-bit delta (it does not lose resolution) yet remains below twice the 32-bit delta (it does not run at a different rate). The test skips on platforms without a 64-bit cycle counter.\nTest steps:\n- Skip unless CONFIG_TIMER_HAS_64BIT_CYCLE_COUNTER is enabled.\n- Sample both the 64-bit and 32-bit cycle counters, sleep 1 ms, then sample again.\n- Compute the 32-bit and 64-bit deltas across the sleep.\nExpected result:\n- The 64-bit delta is >= the 32-bit delta and < twice the 32-bit delta.\nSee also: k_cycle_get_64()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "clock__test_clock_cycle_64"
        }
      ],
      "name": "clock__test_clock_cycle_64",
      "summary": "Verify the 64-bit cycle counter tracks the 32-bit counter over an interval."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/clock--test-clock-uptime",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves the system uptime clock is a forward-progressing time source usable from user mode: both the 64-bit and lower-32-bit uptime readings increase as wall time elapses, the 32-bit reading observes a millisecond boundary crossing, and k_uptime_delta() reports a non-zero elapsed interval between successive calls.\nTest steps:\n- Spin until k_uptime_get() advances by at least 5 ms.\n- Spin until k_uptime_get_32() advances by at least 5 ms.\n- Sample the 32-bit uptime, align to a millisecond boundary, and confirm it grew.\n- Seed a delta reference, then spin until k_uptime_delta() returns non-zero.\n- Spin until k_uptime_ticks() advances, confirming uptime in system ticks.\n- Sample k_uptime_seconds() between two k_uptime_get() readings and confirm it equals the floor of the millisecond uptime in seconds.\nExpected result:\n- Uptime readings increase over time and k_uptime_delta() reports elapsed time.\n- Uptime is also reported in system ticks and in whole seconds, consistent with the millisecond reading.\nSee also: k_uptime_get(), k_uptime_get_32(), k_uptime_delta(), k_uptime_ticks(), k_uptime_seconds()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "clock__test_clock_uptime"
        }
      ],
      "name": "clock__test_clock_uptime",
      "summary": "Verify the uptime clock advances monotonically and reports deltas."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/clock--test-ms-time-duration",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves that k_timer_start() honors a millisecond expiry duration with correct timing semantics: the timer's expiry callback does not fire while less than the configured duration has elapsed, and fires exactly once once the full duration is exceeded, while the stop callback is not invoked on normal expiry.\nTest steps:\n- Start the timer for DURATION ms one-shot and busy-wait LESS_DURATION ms; confirm neither the expiry nor stop counter has incremented.\n- Restart the timer, align to a tick, busy-wait just over DURATION ms, and confirm the expiry counter is exactly 1 and the stop counter is 0.\n- Stop the timer to clean up.\nExpected result:\n- No expiry before the duration elapses; exactly one expiry afterward and no stop-callback invocation.\nSee also: k_timer_start(), k_timer_stop(), k_busy_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "clock__test_ms_time_duration"
        }
      ],
      "name": "clock__test_ms_time_duration",
      "summary": "Verify a kernel timer expires only after its millisecond duration elapses."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/common-1cpu--test-nested-irq-offload",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Exercises the regression where irq_offload() is called from an already-running interrupt (a timer ISR) and the nested handler suspends the interrupted thread, forcing a context switch out of the nested interrupt. Passing proves nested interrupt offload runs the inner handler exactly once and returns control to the correct context without corruption. Skipped on SMP or when nested offload is off.\nTest steps:\n- Raise the test thread priority and start a timer whose ISR calls irq_offload().\n- In a separate thread, spin asserting it is never resumed after the timer fires.\n- The timer ISR invokes the nested offload handler which suspends that thread.\nExpected result:\n- Both the timer handler and the nested offload handler report having executed.\nSee also: irq_offload()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "common_1cpu__test_nested_irq_offload"
        }
      ],
      "name": "common_1cpu__test_nested_irq_offload",
      "summary": "Verify a nested irq_offload() invoked from within an interrupt is handled safely."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/common-errno--test-thread-context",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Confirms that errno is thread-local: concurrent threads assigning distinct errno values, sleeping for different durations to force interleaving, must each still observe their own value when resumed, and the main thread's errno must be unaffected. Passing proves errno is saved and restored per thread on context switch.\nTest steps:\n- Set the main thread's errno to a known value and record it.\n- Spawn N_THREADS threads, each assigning a unique errno then sleeping a staggered interval before re-reading and reporting its result via a FIFO.\n- Collect each thread's pass/fail result and re-check the main thread's errno.\nExpected result:\n- Every thread observes its own errno value and the main thread's errno is unchanged.\nSee also: k_thread_create()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "common_errno__test_thread_context"
        }
      ],
      "name": "common_errno__test_thread_context",
      "summary": "Verify each thread sees a private errno value preserved across context switches."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-broadcast-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A user-mode thread arms the ztest fault hook and calls k_condvar_broadcast() with a NULL pointer. The call must trigger a kernel fault; execution must not fall through to the ztest_test_fail() guard placed after it.\nExpected result:\n- A valid fault is taken at k_condvar_broadcast(NULL); the guard ztest_test_fail() in cond_broadcast_null() is never reached.\nSee also: k_condvar_broadcast()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_broadcast_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_broadcast_null",
