    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-signal-wakes-highest-priority",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Three helper threads block on the same condition variable at distinct, lower priorities than the test thread. The test signals once per iteration and each woken thread appends its id to wake_order[]. The wakeups must follow priority order (highest priority first), not creation/FIFO order.\nExpected result:\n- wake_order[] is the waiter ids in descending-priority order.\nNote: Skipped when CONFIG_MP_MAX_NUM_CPUS > 1: with multiple CPUs waiters can run concurrently, so a strict wakeup order is not guaranteed.\nSee also: k_condvar_signal()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_signal_wakes_highest_priority"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_signal_wakes_highest_priority",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_signal() wakes the highest-priority waiter first."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-signal-wakes-one",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "TOTAL_THREADS_WAITING (3) helper threads all block on the same condition variable with K_FOREVER. A single k_condvar_signal() must release exactly one of them; the others must stay blocked. This is the defining difference between signal and broadcast and was previously only covered for broadcast.\nExpected result:\n- k_condvar_signal() returns 0.\n- Exactly one waiter runs past k_condvar_wait() (woken_count == 1); the rest remain blocked until a later broadcast drains them.\nSee also: k_condvar_signal()\nSee also: k_condvar_broadcast()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_signal_wakes_one"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_signal_wakes_one",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_signal() releases exactly one of several waiters."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-static-define",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "static_condvar is created with K_CONDVAR_DEFINE() and never passed to k_condvar_init(). The test exercises signal/broadcast with no waiters and a full wait/signal round-trip to confirm the static initializer produces a usable object.\nExpected result:\n- k_condvar_signal()/k_condvar_broadcast() with no waiter return 0.\n- A blocked k_condvar_wait() is released by a signal and returns 0.\nSee also: K_CONDVAR_DEFINE\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_static_define"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_static_define",
      "summary": "Verify a statically defined condition variable works without init."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-usecase-broadcast",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Same producer/consumer scenario as test_condvar_usecase_signal, but the incrementer threads release the watcher with k_condvar_broadcast() instead of k_condvar_signal(). With a single watcher the observable outcome is identical; this exercises the broadcast wakeup path in the same use case.\nExpected result:\n- Final count equals 145 (verified by zassert_equal() in _condvar_usecase()).\nSee also: k_condvar_broadcast()\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_usecase_broadcast"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_usecase_broadcast",
      "summary": "Verify a producer/consumer hand-off driven by k_condvar_broadcast()."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-usecase-signal",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "One watcher thread holds the mutex and waits on the condition variable until a shared counter reaches COUNT_LIMIT; two incrementer threads raise the counter under the same mutex and call k_condvar_signal() when the limit is hit. The deterministic final counter value proves the single-waiter wakeup fired and the mutex-protected shared state stayed consistent.\nExpected result:\n- Final count equals 145: 20 increments from the two incrementer threads (2 x TCOUNT) plus 125 added by the watcher after it is released (verified by zassert_equal() in _condvar_usecase()).\nSee also: k_condvar_signal()\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_usecase_signal"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_usecase_signal",
      "summary": "Verify a producer/consumer hand-off driven by k_condvar_signal()."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-forever",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A helper thread calls k_condvar_wait() with K_FOREVER and no thread ever signals the condition variable. The waiter must remain blocked. The test confirms this explicitly by joining the helper with a finite deadline: the join must time out, proving the thread is still alive and blocked. The waiter is then aborted.\nTest steps:\n- Start a helper thread that calls k_condvar_wait(..., K_FOREVER).\n- k_thread_join() the helper with a finite deadline.\nExpected result:\n- k_thread_join() returns -EAGAIN (the deadline expires while the helper is still blocked). A return of 0 would mean a spurious wakeup let the helper terminate, which condvar_wait_task() also catches with zassert_unreachable().\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()\nSee also: k_thread_join()\nTest k_condvar_wait() forever",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_forever"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_forever",
      "summary": "Verify a K_FOREVER waiter stays blocked when no signal is sent."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-forever-wake",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A helper thread locks a mutex and calls k_condvar_wait() with a K_FOREVER timeout, so it blocks indefinitely. A second thread then calls k_condvar_signal() on the same condition variable. The test verifies that the signal wakes the blocked waiter, which returns 0 (woken, not timed out).\nTest steps:\n- Initialize the condition variable.\n- Start a waiter thread that calls k_condvar_wait(..., K_FOREVER).\n- Start a waker thread that calls k_condvar_signal().\nExpected result:\n- k_condvar_wait() in the waiter returns 0 (verified in condvar_wait_wake_task()).\n- k_condvar_signal() returns 0.\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()\nSee also: k_condvar_signal()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_forever_wake"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_forever_wake",
      "summary": "Verify a thread blocked forever is released by k_condvar_signal()."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-forever-wake-from-isr",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A helper thread blocks in k_condvar_wait() with K_FOREVER. The test then issues k_condvar_signal() from an ISR via irq_offload(). The test verifies the signal raised in interrupt context releases the waiter, which returns 0.\nExpected result:\n- k_condvar_wait() returns 0 (verified in condvar_wait_wake_task()).\nNote: This is a kernel-mode test (ZTEST, not ZTEST_USER) because signalling is performed from an ISR.\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()\nSee also: k_condvar_signal()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_forever_wake_from_isr"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_forever_wake_from_isr",
      "summary": "Verify a thread blocked forever is woken by a signal from ISR context."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-nowait",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A helper thread calls k_condvar_wait() with a timeout of 0 ticks. With no signal pending, the call must not block and must return -EAGAIN immediately.\nExpected result:\n- k_condvar_wait() returns -EAGAIN (verified in condvar_wait_task()).\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_nowait"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_nowait",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_wait() with a zero timeout returns -EAGAIN immediately."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-nowait-keeps-mutex",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "k_condvar_wait() with K_NO_WAIT returns -EAGAIN without touching the mutex (a distinct code path from the timeout path). This test locks a mutex, makes the no-wait call, then unlocks to prove the caller still owns it.\nExpected result:\n- k_condvar_wait() returns -EAGAIN.\n- k_mutex_unlock() succeeds (returns -EPERM if ownership was lost).\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()\nSee also: k_mutex_unlock()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_nowait_keeps_mutex"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_nowait_keeps_mutex",
      "summary": "Verify the K_NO_WAIT path of k_condvar_wait() leaves the mutex held."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-null",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A user-mode thread arms the ztest fault hook and calls k_condvar_wait() with NULL condition variable and mutex pointers. The call must trigger a kernel fault; execution must not fall through to the ztest_test_fail() guard placed after it.\nExpected result:\n- A valid fault is taken at k_condvar_wait(NULL, NULL, ...); the guard ztest_test_fail() in cond_wait_null() is never reached.\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_null"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_null",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_wait(NULL, NULL, ...) faults when called from user mode."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-recursive-mutex-not-released",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Unlike POSIX pthread_cond_wait(), which releases a recursively locked mutex completely and restores the count on return, k_condvar_wait() calls k_mutex_unlock() exactly once. When the mutex is locked recursively the count is only decremented, so the waiting thread keeps ownership across the wait.\nThe test locks a mutex twice, then waits on a condition variable. While it is blocked, a contender thread tries to acquire the mutex with a short timeout and must fail, proving the mutex was not released. A separate thread signals the wait afterwards so the test can unwind cleanly.\nExpected result:\n- The contender's k_mutex_lock() times out with -EAGAIN (current behaviour).\n- k_condvar_wait() returns 0 once signaled and both recursive levels unlock.\nNote: KNOWN LIMITATION / divergence from POSIX. If kernel/condvar.c is changed to fully release a recursively locked mutex, this expectation must be updated (the contender would then succeed).\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()\nSee also: k_mutex_lock()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_recursive_mutex_not_released"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_recursive_mutex_not_released",
      "summary": "Document that k_condvar_wait() does not fully release a recursive mutex."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-signaled-keeps-mutex",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Complements the timeout-relock test for the success path: a waker thread signals the condition variable while the test thread is blocked in k_condvar_wait(). On return the mutex must be re-acquired by the caller.\nExpected result:\n- k_condvar_wait() returns 0 (signaled).\n- k_mutex_unlock() succeeds (returns -EPERM if the mutex was not re-locked).\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()\nSee also: k_mutex_unlock()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_signaled_keeps_mutex"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_signaled_keeps_mutex",
      "summary": "Verify a signaled k_condvar_wait() returns with the mutex held."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-timeout",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A helper thread calls k_condvar_wait() with a 50 ms timeout and no thread ever signals the condition variable. The test verifies the wait fails with -EAGAIN once the timeout elapses.\nExpected result:\n- k_condvar_wait() returns -EAGAIN (verified in condvar_wait_task()).\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_timeout"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_timeout",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_wait() returns -EAGAIN when its timeout expires."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wait-timeout-relocks-mutex",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Per the contract documented in kernel/condvar.c, k_condvar_wait() must always return with the mutex held by the calling thread, even when the wait times out (matching POSIX semantics for pthread_cond_timedwait). The test locks a mutex, waits with a short timeout and no waker so the wait expires, then unlocks the mutex to prove ownership was restored.\nExpected result:\n- k_condvar_wait() returns -EAGAIN on timeout.\n- k_mutex_unlock() succeeds; a regression that failed to re-lock the mutex would make it return -EPERM (mutex not locked by the calling thread).\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()\nSee also: k_mutex_unlock()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_timeout_relocks_mutex"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wait_timeout_relocks_mutex",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_wait() re-locks the mutex when the wait times out."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wake-before-timeout",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "A helper thread calls k_condvar_wait() with a 100 ms timeout and a second thread signals the condition variable immediately. The signal arrives before the timeout expires, so the waiter must return 0 (woken), not -EAGAIN.\nExpected result:\n- k_condvar_wait() returns 0 (verified in condvar_wait_wake_task()).\n- k_condvar_signal() returns 0.\nSee also: k_condvar_wait()\nSee also: k_condvar_signal()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wake_before_timeout"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wake_before_timeout",
      "summary": "Verify a finite-timeout waiter is woken by a signal before it times out."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-condvar-wake-no-waiters",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Exercises the no-waiter branches: with an empty wait queue, signal and broadcast must be no-ops that report zero woken threads.\nExpected result:\n- k_condvar_signal() returns 0.\n- k_condvar_broadcast() returns 0.\nSee also: k_condvar_signal()\nSee also: k_condvar_broadcast()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wake_no_waiters"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_condvar_wake_no_waiters",
      "summary": "Verify k_condvar_signal()/k_condvar_broadcast() with no waiters return 0."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/condvar-tests--test-multiple-condvar-wait-wake",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "TOTAL_THREADS_WAITING (3) helper threads each block on a distinct condition variable (multiple_condvar[i]). A matching set of waker threads each wakes its own condition variable. With one waiter per condition variable, each wake must release exactly one thread, demonstrating that condition variables are independent and do not cross-signal.\nExpected result:\n- Each wake call releases exactly one waiter (returns 1).\n- Each k_condvar_wait() returns 0 (verified in condvar_multiple_wait_wake_task()).\nNote: Easily confused with test_condvar_multiple_threads_wait_wake, which has many waiters on a SINGLE condition variable. This one is the \"one condition variable per waiter\" case. Because woken is 1, condvar_multiple_wake_task() always takes its k_condvar_broadcast() branch, so its k_condvar_signal() path is not exercised here.\nSee also: k_condvar_signal()\nSee also: k_condvar_broadcast()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "condvar_tests__test_multiple_condvar_wait_wake"
        }
      ],
      "name": "condvar_tests__test_multiple_condvar_wait_wake",
      "summary": "Verify independent condition variables each wake their own single waiter."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/constructor--test-constructor",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "Passing proves that functions marked with the GCC constructor attribute are invoked during system startup before the test runs, and that prioritized constructors execute in ascending priority order ahead of unprioritized ones.\nTest steps:\n- Register three constructors that each record an identifier on invocation: priority 101, priority 1000, and one with no explicit priority.\n- In the test body, inspect the recorded count and ordering.\nExpected result:\n- All three constructors ran (count is 3), priority 101 ran first, priority 1000 ran second, and the unprioritized constructor ran last.\nSee also: ZTEST_SUITE()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "constructor__test_constructor"
        }
      ],
      "name": "constructor__test_constructor",
      "summary": "Verify C constructors run before main in priority order."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/context-cpu-idle--test-cpu-idle",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The architecture provides an idle primitive that suspends the CPU when there is no work to do and resumes on the next interrupt. The test enters k_cpu_idle() and confirms the CPU stayed idle for the expected duration before an interrupt woke it.\nTest steps:\n- Record the system time before idling.\n- Enter k_cpu_idle() and wait for an interrupt to resume execution.\n- On wake-up, record the system time again and compare the elapsed time.\nExpected result:\n- The CPU idles and resumes after the expected time has elapsed.\nSee also: k_cpu_idle()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "context_cpu_idle__test_cpu_idle"
        }
      ],
      "name": "context_cpu_idle__test_cpu_idle",
      "summary": "Verify k_cpu_idle() idles the CPU until an interrupt wakes it."
    },
    {
      "type": "functionalsafety_RequirementVerification",
      "spdxId": "zephyr:verifications/context-cpu-idle--test-cpu-idle-atomic",
      "functionalsafety_verificationMethod": [
        "spdx:FunctionalSafety/VerificationType/test"
      ],
      "creationInfo": "zephyr:creationinfo",
      "description": "The architecture provides an atomic idle primitive that suspends the CPU with interrupts locked and resumes when an interrupt arrives, without losing a wake-up that races the idle. The test arms a timer, enters k_cpu_atomic_idle(), and confirms the CPU stayed idle for the expected duration and was woken by the timer.\nTest steps:\n- Record the system time before idling.\n- Lock interrupts, arm a timer, and enter k_cpu_atomic_idle().\n- On wake-up, record the system time again and compare against the timer.\nExpected result:\n- The CPU idles until the timer fires and the elapsed time matches the timer duration within tolerance.\nSee also: k_cpu_atomic_idle()",
      "externalIdentifier": [
        {
          "type": "ExternalIdentifier",
          "comment": "ztest case",
          "externalIdentifierType": "other",
          "identifier": "context_cpu_idle__test_cpu_idle_atomic"
        }
      ],
      "name": "context_cpu_idle__test_cpu_idle_atomic",
      "summary": "Verify k_cpu_atomic_idle() idles the CPU until an interrupt wakes it."
    },
    {
